From 8c5f9dcff9eec70b0694f6fadd9de5b6676681bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: facetimehd downstream <facetimehd-downstream@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 08/19] isp: survive a firmware command that never completes

Two problems, both reachable from ordinary use.

A command that timed out freed its request object immediately. Firmware that
is merely slow, not dead, may still write the completion afterwards - into
memory the allocator has by then handed to someone else. On a timeout the
object is now deliberately leaked onto dev_priv->mem_objects, which keeps
its offset reserved, and reclaimed in bulk by isp_mem_destroy_all() the next
time the ISP is torn down. The device is marked wedged so every further
command fails with -EIO in a few instructions instead of piling up more
two-second timeouts, and fthd_pm_down() clears the flag once the hardware
and every outstanding object are gone.

The ring waits were interruptible. A submitted command cannot be cancelled,
so a signal that interrupted the completion wait left STREAMOFF releasing
VB2 and IOMMU mappings that firmware still owned - visible as a failed
channel stop, unknown buffer tags, and DMA writes to unmapped address zero
when a capturing application was killed. The waits stay bounded but become
uninterruptible, and the completion paths use wake_up() rather than
wake_up_interruptible(), which cannot wake an uninterruptible waiter.

fthd_mark_firmware_wedged() also errors the VB2 queue, so a reader blocked
in DQBUF or poll() is woken with an error rather than left hanging. It is
used by the three places that discover a dead device: the command timeout, a
stuck interrupt (which now masks the interrupt properly instead of calling
free_irq() from the work item it runs in), and PCI AER/DPC error recovery,
which gets an err_handler for exactly that purpose.

fthd_isp_cmd() and fthd_isp_debug_cmd() were near-identical copies of one
another; they become thin wrappers over a shared body, differing only in
channel, timeout and the two behaviours the debug channel actually has.
---
